WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.034 --> 00:00:02.035
[dramatic music]

00:00:02.101 --> 00:00:03.369
 Chris, John, congratulations.

00:00:03.435 --> 00:00:04.704
You guys have made it
into the third round

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:06.005
of this competition.

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:07.540
Now we're sending you
back to your home forges

00:00:07.606 --> 00:00:13.712
to recreate this iconic
weapon from American history--

00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:15.015
General Patton's saber.

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:18.618
Good luck.

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:19.952
We'll see you in four days.

00:00:20.019 --> 00:00:20.686
 Good luck.

00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:21.421
 All right.

00:00:25.491 --> 00:00:29.395
 Today, I'm going to build the
actual blade part of the saber.

00:00:29.461 --> 00:00:32.198
I'm making a ladder
pattern Damascus billet.

00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:36.669
I'm stacking 1085 and 15N20.

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:39.272
So ladder pattern Damascus,
it's a simple pattern.

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:41.640
I'm going to weld it,
put some layers into it,

00:00:41.707 --> 00:00:46.146
and then cut it with the chop
saw, and then draw it out.

00:00:46.246 --> 00:00:49.949
This is a large blade, and there
can be a lot of things that go

00:00:50.015 --> 00:00:52.051
wrong with this much forging.

00:00:52.118 --> 00:00:54.520
So I'm not comfortable
putting all my eggs just

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:55.722
in that one billet.

00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:58.858
So my plan is to
make a 1560 billet

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:00.760
so I have insurance
that I'll have

00:01:00.826 --> 00:01:02.629
a good blade for this billet.

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:03.496
I have lots of eggs.

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:04.263
I got tons of eggs.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.098
Do you guys need some eggs?

00:01:05.164 --> 00:01:08.401 line:7%
Anybody want eggs?

00:01:08.501 --> 00:01:12.071 line:0%
CHRIS: Today I hope to
get the blade quenched,

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:14.874 line:0%
so I need to get
this fire going.

00:01:14.941 --> 00:01:16.843
Hmm.

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:20.313
I've picked up a little bit
of a curve in this thing.

00:01:20.379 --> 00:01:24.483
I do notice that it's not an
exact even heat-up in the fire.

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:27.686
There's more heat towards
either end than in the middle.

00:01:27.753 --> 00:01:30.056
I don't want to waste too
much more time chasing it out.

00:01:30.122 --> 00:01:31.457
There's always a chance
that a lot of it'll

00:01:31.523 --> 00:01:35.127
come back out once I
dip it in the quench.

00:01:35.227 --> 00:01:37.663
I believe my blade
has gotten hot enough.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.299
I am ready to quench.

00:01:42.835 --> 00:01:46.939
While my blade was heating up,
it wasn't an exact even heat-up

00:01:47.039 --> 00:01:48.507
in the fire.

00:01:48.607 --> 00:01:51.878
So I do still have a fairly
significant S-curve warp to it.

00:01:51.978 --> 00:01:55.448
So I quickly throw it between
a couple of big chunks of wood

00:01:55.515 --> 00:01:58.184
to see if I can
address the warp.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:00.386
If this doesn't work,
I'm screwed now.

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:04.657
OK.

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:08.461
I've got a very, very slight
warble right towards the end,

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:11.097
but I'm positive I can take
it out with the grinder.

00:02:11.163 --> 00:02:14.701
So we might be in good shape.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:17.303
I feel pretty good about that.

00:02:17.369 --> 00:02:19.906 line:0%
I think I'm getting
very slightly soft close

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:23.643 line:0%
to the hilt. Rather than go
through the quench process

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:25.478 line:0%
again, I think I'm just
going to go with it,

00:02:25.578 --> 00:02:28.781 line:0%
keep my fingers crossed,
and pray to the gods.

00:02:28.881 --> 00:02:31.317 line:0%
JOHN: This morning, I'll
figure out which blade is going

00:02:31.383 --> 00:02:33.185 line:7%
to be worthy of going forward.

00:02:33.285 --> 00:02:36.489
I really want the
Damascus to be my sword.

00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:40.826
So I start grinding on
it, and right away I start

00:02:40.893 --> 00:02:42.728
seeing some de-laminations.

00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:46.933
In this edge here, they're
wanting to fall apart.

00:02:46.999 --> 00:02:50.670
I'm going to scrap the Damascus,
save it for the back strap,

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:54.740
and move on to the 5160,
and make that my blade.

00:02:54.807 --> 00:02:58.511
I'm not going to take a risk
with $10,000 on the line.

00:02:58.578 --> 00:03:02.481
I go to the forge, get it hot,
and I go in the quench chamber.

00:03:02.581 --> 00:03:04.751 line:0%
When I pull this
knife out of the oil,

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:08.020 line:7%
I have a nice straight sword.

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:09.555 line:0%
CHRIS: It's the
morning of day four.

00:03:09.621 --> 00:03:11.790
I think I've got myself set
up really well for a final day

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:13.759
of construction and refinement.

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:17.330
Once I have my wood-block
shape for my handle,

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:19.064
I start figuring
out how I'm going

00:03:19.164 --> 00:03:20.867
to try to make this back strap.

00:03:20.967 --> 00:03:23.202
I got this old
kind-of-cool-looking

00:03:23.302 --> 00:03:25.104
spoon of unknown metal.

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:27.406
I think it's some
sort of steel plated

00:03:27.473 --> 00:03:29.108
in some sort of other metal.

00:03:29.208 --> 00:03:31.143
But it looks cool,
and I'm going to beat,

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:32.978
and bash, and bend
it until it fits

00:03:33.078 --> 00:03:34.180
around the back of my hand.

00:03:34.247 --> 00:03:35.448
All right.

00:03:35.514 --> 00:03:38.051
I get it glued up to
the rest of the sword.

00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:40.119
Now, once my epoxy sets,
it's going to be time

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:43.323
to moving on to sharpening.

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:49.528
This blade is sharp as a razor,
and it's ready for testing.

00:03:49.628 --> 00:03:52.966 line:7%
It will cut.

00:03:53.032 --> 00:03:54.400 line:0%
JOHN: Today I got
to put everything

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:58.270 line:0%
together, put the final edge
on the sword, and test it.

00:03:58.337 --> 00:04:00.206
So it's time to get
the guard started.

00:04:00.272 --> 00:04:02.307
So I go up to the plasma cutter.

00:04:02.374 --> 00:04:04.443
I'm gonna cut out a
piece of sheet metal

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.345
that's going to
become our guard.

00:04:06.445 --> 00:04:07.513
I'm a horseshoer.

00:04:07.614 --> 00:04:08.214
I have horses.

00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:09.315
Put a horse on it--

00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:10.883
horse head for
the cavalry sword.

00:04:10.984 --> 00:04:16.088
This is my version of the
cowboy's cavalry sword.

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:19.025
Say that twice.

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:20.425
I'm going to acid
etch the guard black.

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:22.528
And then I'm going to put
some enamel over the top

00:04:22.628 --> 00:04:25.765
just to preserve it, and then
line the inside of the guard

00:04:25.831 --> 00:04:27.267
with pig leather.

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:29.035
That'll make sure it's nice
and comfy for their hands.

00:04:29.135 --> 00:04:32.371
I finish assembling the sword
just with enough daylight

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:33.506
to be able to test it.

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:37.810
This is Whiskey.

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:41.281
[heavy metal music]

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:47.319
Hey, it worked.

00:04:47.419 --> 00:04:49.421
I'm finished building
this crazy sword.

00:04:49.521 --> 00:04:51.557
Why not go out with a bang?

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:52.558
[explosion]

00:04:55.528 --> 00:04:57.429
'Merica!

00:04:57.496 --> 00:04:59.264
DOUG MARCAIDA: Welcome
to the kill test.

00:04:59.331 --> 00:05:01.134
Your sabers look deadly.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.768
To find that out, I
will take your weapon,

00:05:02.835 --> 00:05:06.972
deliver some slashes and thrusts
on this ballistics dummy.

00:05:07.072 --> 00:05:07.874
John, you're up first.

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:09.675
You ready for this?

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:12.044
 Yeah.

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:15.982
[heavy metal music]

00:05:26.325 --> 00:05:28.461
 [laughing]

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:45.578
Cut its heart out.

00:05:49.282 --> 00:05:51.483
It's just not right.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:53.152
DOUG MARCAIDA: All
right, John, first up,

00:05:53.219 --> 00:05:54.386
let's talk about the
handle construction.

00:05:54.487 --> 00:05:56.322
It's a little bit
on the big side,

00:05:56.388 --> 00:05:58.490
but I'm able to hold on to it.

00:05:58.557 --> 00:06:02.494
Now, your edge--
it's very sharp,

00:06:02.561 --> 00:06:06.098
and it is a pleasure to wield
because aside from looking

00:06:06.198 --> 00:06:09.067
beautiful, it'll kill.

00:06:09.134 --> 00:06:10.302
 Thank you very much.

00:06:10.369 --> 00:06:11.671
DOUG MARCAIDA: All
right, Chris, your turn.

00:06:11.738 --> 00:06:12.338
You ready, sir?

00:06:12.405 --> 00:06:13.138
 Yes sir.

00:06:13.205 --> 00:06:13.873
I sure am.

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:21.981
[heavy metal music]

00:06:33.158 --> 00:06:33.860
 Phew.

00:06:46.705 --> 00:06:48.908
 All right, Chris, let's
talk about your saber here.

00:06:49.008 --> 00:06:52.812
First up, what I love about
it, it's got a sharp edge.

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:54.480
Every swing I do cuts.

00:06:54.547 --> 00:06:56.516
The one thing here
is that you did

00:06:56.582 --> 00:07:00.252
pick up a little bit of a bend
right here at this junction.

00:07:00.319 --> 00:07:02.120
It's warped a little bit.

00:07:02.187 --> 00:07:04.423
But it pierces and cuts.

00:07:04.489 --> 00:07:07.860
And most importantly,
it will kill.

00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:08.961
 Thank you, sir.

00:07:09.028 --> 00:07:10.997
 All right, bladesmiths,
for our strength test,

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:12.631
I'm going to take your sabers.

00:07:12.698 --> 00:07:14.633
And I'm going to beat
them and stab them

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.836
into these field obstacles.

00:07:16.902 --> 00:07:19.104
Now, I'm not really concerned
about what your swords

00:07:19.171 --> 00:07:20.639
do to our obstacles.

00:07:20.706 --> 00:07:22.575
I want to see the opposite--
what they do to your swords.

00:07:22.675 --> 00:07:23.409
John, you're up first.

00:07:23.476 --> 00:07:24.143
You ready to go?

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.412
 Ready to go.

00:07:26.479 --> 00:07:28.581
[dramatic music]

00:07:32.418 --> 00:07:33.920
 [grunt]

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:37.457
[grunt]

00:07:39.958 --> 00:07:40.860
[grunt]

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:45.798
[grunt]

00:07:46.832 --> 00:07:47.733
[grunt]

00:07:53.772 --> 00:07:54.707
[grunt]

00:08:02.515 --> 00:08:03.516
Nice job, John.

00:08:03.582 --> 00:08:04.883
I like the blade.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:07.386
The sword itself overall
is on the heavy side.

00:08:07.486 --> 00:08:09.555
The handle is a lot there--

00:08:09.655 --> 00:08:10.956
a lot of extra weight.

00:08:11.023 --> 00:08:13.425
It does help counterbalance
quite a bit, though.

00:08:13.492 --> 00:08:14.760
Still got a good edge on it.

00:08:14.827 --> 00:08:16.262
There was some pretty
heavy strikes on there.

00:08:16.362 --> 00:08:18.730
And the blade bent
but came back to true.

00:08:18.797 --> 00:08:20.766
It's all right,
tight, and straight.

00:08:20.866 --> 00:08:21.299
Good job.

00:08:21.366 --> 00:08:22.200
 Thank you.

00:08:22.267 --> 00:08:23.903
 How you feeling, Chris?

00:08:24.003 --> 00:08:24.970
 Good as I can be, sir.

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:26.806
J. NEILSON: All right.

00:08:26.873 --> 00:08:28.774
[dramatic music]

00:08:35.013 --> 00:08:35.681
[shout]

00:08:35.748 --> 00:08:36.282
 Whoa!

00:08:40.552 --> 00:08:41.954
 [inaudible] right here.

00:08:42.054 --> 00:08:43.789
MEDICAL MAN: Yeah.

00:08:43.856 --> 00:08:44.356
You're good.

00:08:44.456 --> 00:08:45.191
 Oh [bleep].

00:08:48.828 --> 00:08:50.495
MEDICAL MAN: Good--
no cuts, no blood.

00:08:50.595 --> 00:08:52.665
CHRIS: [raspberry]

00:08:52.765 --> 00:08:55.701
J. NEILSON: Lucky day.

00:08:55.768 --> 00:08:56.502
 [sigh]

00:08:56.569 --> 00:08:59.272
[dramatic music]

00:08:59.338 --> 00:09:02.474
Chris, your blade has suffered
a catastrophic failure

00:09:02.574 --> 00:09:03.943
in our strength test.

00:09:04.009 --> 00:09:07.046
And unfortunately, you
cannot continue with testing,

00:09:07.146 --> 00:09:09.949
which means you cannot be the
"Forged in Fire" champion.

00:09:10.049 --> 00:09:11.116
Please exit the forge.

00:09:11.183 --> 00:09:13.619
Come on forward, pal.

00:09:13.686 --> 00:09:17.056
 I did have a small issue
when it got to my heat treat.

00:09:17.122 --> 00:09:17.990
Sorry about that.

00:09:18.057 --> 00:09:19.091
 That's all right, man.

00:09:19.158 --> 00:09:20.626
CHRIS: I didn't want to
risk a second quench,

00:09:20.726 --> 00:09:22.160
because I didn't want to
make it overly brittle.

00:09:22.227 --> 00:09:24.830
I do have some regret about
that decision at this point.

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:26.699
Of course I would have liked
to be the "Forged in Fire"

00:09:26.766 --> 00:09:29.935
champion, but I am really proud
of myself as a weapons maker.

00:09:30.001 --> 00:09:33.039
And I plan on getting right back
in my forge when I get home,

00:09:33.105 --> 00:09:35.074
and I'm going to
move forward as ever.

00:09:37.743 --> 00:09:40.346
 Well, John, General Patton
was known for being a tough guy,

00:09:40.446 --> 00:09:43.115
and you made a tough
and sharp sword

00:09:43.182 --> 00:09:44.617
that really honors his name.

00:09:44.684 --> 00:09:47.052
Congratulations, you are the
"Forged in Fire" champion,

00:09:47.119 --> 00:09:49.287
and that's a title that comes
with a check for $10,000.

00:09:49.387 --> 00:09:50.556
Good job.
[applause]

00:09:50.656 --> 00:09:51.423
 Thank you.

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:52.624
Thank you.

00:09:52.691 --> 00:09:53.826
WIL WILLIS: How do
you feel right now?

00:09:53.893 --> 00:09:54.560
 Oh yeah!

00:09:54.627 --> 00:09:55.594
 All right, man.

00:09:55.661 --> 00:09:57.429
Come on over and
shake our hands.

00:09:57.529 --> 00:09:58.296
Good job, brother.

00:09:58.363 --> 00:09:59.297
It's a beautiful sword.

00:09:59.364 --> 00:10:00.533
 Yeah!

00:10:00.633 --> 00:10:01.867
I'm the new "Forged
in Fire" champion.

00:10:01.934 --> 00:10:04.169
 You did a phenomenal
job on that heat treat.

00:10:04.236 --> 00:10:05.438
 Thank you, sir.

00:10:05.504 --> 00:10:07.839
I'm proud of what I did,
and been a fun deal.

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:10.509
Hey, can I get a "Forged
in Fire" belt buckle now?

00:10:10.576 --> 00:10:11.276
No belt buckle?

00:10:11.376 --> 00:10:13.112
Come on.

